  "surprise": "662 at 8 loops (seeds 662/674/651) against 690: -28, and below the 2-loop diagnostic on every seed (676/682/652, mean 670), so metric minus diag went from 502's +18 (23/19/13) to -8 (-14/-8/-1). The pre-registered falsifier fired: after RL, 8 loops no longer beat 2. Per-sample dev accuracy 0.433/0.404/0.402 at 8 loops against 0.427/0.417/0.403 at 2 (502: 0.466/0.489/0.460 against 0.435/0.461/0.436). The 2-loop theorem count is unchanged (670 against 672) though its per-sample accuracy is lower, so in theorems the whole loss is the depth benefit. The throughput half of the prediction held and bought nothing: 4,858/4,780/4,774 pretraining steps (1.40x, as benchmarked) left val loss at 2 loops at 0.0898/0.0830/0.0852 (mean 0.0860 against 0.0866; predicted at or below 0.085), and 9/9/8 RL rounds against 7/7/6 solved 1,209 targets against 1,203. At equal round count the fine-tuned model was behind: 1-loop accuracy on RL targets 0.696/0.681 after 6 fine-tunes on seeds 0/1 against 0.73/0.74, and 0.677 against 0.731 after 5 on seed 2. The retry comparison shows where depth went. On the pretrained model deeper retries still helped (round-1 per-sample acceptance at 2/4/8 loops 0.045/0.069/0.089, 0.049/0.078/0.077, 0.014/0.020/0.026 by seed) and pretraining val loss at 8 loops was at or below 2 loops on every seed, so truncation did not break deep passes in pretraining. After fine-tuning the advantage was gone: pooled over seeds and rounds, resistant targets were cracked 11.4% at 2 loops, 12.7% at 4 and 12.6% at 8 (502: 11.4/13.8/15.9), and in rounds 3+ 9.2/9.6/9.3 (502: 10.4/11.7/14.3). Interpretation, not established: the premise that passes before the last two carry no useful gradient is wrong. With truncation the first core pass, which does most of the work (1 loop reaches 0.72 on RL targets), is differentiated only at loop counts 1 and 2, a quarter of the batches, so each step teaches the shared core less; and the +18 that 8 loops buy in 500 and 502 looks like something full backprop through the unrolled passes builds on the long RL proofs. Next: if throughput is wanted, narrow the loop range with full backprop (the spike's 1-4 arm) instead of cutting the gradient; if depth is wanted, the lever is more end-to-end gradient through deep unrolls on hard proofs, not less.",
